Q1.
BEFORE (the note's executive summary and its status table, before (accepted as correct)): We apply \emph{unbinned machine-learning unfolding} (OmniFold~\cite{Andreassen:2019cjw, Andreassen:2021zzk}) to MINERvA's medium-energy neutrino data. The principal result is the sixteen-cell cross section of \S\ref{sec:fived16}, with its covariance and generator comparison. The results hold for this implementation, one data set, and the configurations, observables and deformations tested (\S\ref{sec:intro}). The generator tests are an application that illustrates calibrated inference with a biased estimator. \paragraph{Results overview:} \begin{itemize}[itemsep=2pt, parsep=0pt, topsep=2pt] \item \textbf{Reproduction.} Unbinned $d^{2}\sigma/(d\pT\,d\pPar)$ reproduces the published integrated cross section~\cite{MINERvA:2021owq} within about one percent, with a correlated shape difference. The total cross-section ratio is $\ratioTot$, and \SI{\binsTen}{\percent} of bins are within \SI{10}{\percent}. A separately constructed uncertainty is comparable to the published one: its per-bin median is \SI{\uqMedian}{\percent} against \SI{\uqPaper}{\percent}, and its integral \SI{\uqIntegral}{\percent} against \SI{\uqPaperIntegral}{\percent}. Its flux band carries MAT's $\nu$--$e$ and inverse-muon-decay constraint weights (\S\ref{sec:results-uq}). A generator-shape model-dependence component, reported beside that uncertainty and absent from the published one, raises the per-bin median to \SI{\qrCmodelMedian}{\percent} (\S\ref{sec:uq-2d-scope}). The central value and that uncertainty share the LightGBM estimator, with refined negative-weight background subtraction. LightGBM was chosen over the exact-split estimator, which is kept as a cross-check, after both comparisons with the publication were known (\S\ref{sec:method-config}). Against the full published covariance, $\chi^{2}/\mathrm{ndf}=\chiPaper$ (exact-split: $\chiPaperExact$) is a descriptive distance. Its source has not been identified (\S\ref{sec:results-tension}). \item \textbf{Unbinned 3D/4D/5D MINERvA unfolds.} Adding $\Eavail$, $q_{3}$ and $W$ gives simultaneous unfolds. In configuration (a), the 4D and 5D marginal-normalization checks against the lower-dimensional result pass to $\approx$1--2\,\%. The configuration-(e) 3D marginal agrees with the configuration-(f) 2D result to \SI{0.141}{\percent} in total, a comparison across configurations (\S\ref{sec:3d}). Binned MINERvA measurements already reach triple-differential~\cite{MINERvA:2022qelike3d,MINERvA:2026qeleme}. The 3D and 4D injected-bump closures, of configuration (a), leave 0.10 of the injected $\Eavail$ change and close $q_{3}$ to \SI{1.1}{\percent}. Neither closure is graded (\S\ref{sec:3d-closure}, Table~\ref{tab:closure-map}). Reconstructed $W$ is fixed by $(\pT,\pPar,q_{3})$, so the five are simultaneous, not independent, observables (\S\ref{sec:eavailw-definitions}). $\Eavail$ keeps the inherited \SI{135}{MeV} charged-pion constant (\S\ref{sec:eavail-definition}). \item \textbf{Low-$\Eavail$ central values (descriptive).} All four truth-level generators, GENIE~\cite{Andreopoulos:2009rq}, MINERvA Tune~\cite{MINERvA:2016lowrecoil}, NuWro~\cite{Golan:2012wx} and GiBUU~\cite{Buss:2011mx}, under-predict the unfolded central value in the quasielastic--$\Delta$ dip ($0.1\le\Eavail<\SI{0.4}{GeV}$), the region of the established MINERvA low-recoil discrepancy~\cite{MINERvA:2016lowrecoil,MINERvA:2022incl}. The GENIE and NuWro predictions are flux-repaired (\S\ref{sec:3d-models}). The comparisons with the tested pion-FSI dials and with Valencia 2p2h~\cite{Nieves:2011pp,Gran:2013kda} are in \S\ref{sec:3d-2p2h} (Fig.~\ref{fig:3dmodels}). \item \textbf{High-$\Eavail$, high-$W$ excess (descriptive).} In cell-integrated terms, most of the positive difference from the Tune~v1 prior lies at $\Eavail\ge\SI{0.8}{GeV}$, much of it at $W\ge\SI{1.8}{GeV}$. A fifth of the positive difference sits in the single widest overflow cell. The high-$\Eavail$, high-$W$ region carries the largest regularization bias measured in \S\ref{sec:5d}. In ratio, the excess also extends to low $W$. The four external generators also fall below the data in this corner, each by about its overall shortfall once GiBUU's sample limit is allowed for (\S\ref{sec:eavailw-band}). The NuWro and GiBUU ratios contain an uncorrected carbon-versus-CH target difference (\S\ref{sec:eavailw-generators}). \item \textbf{Application: joint five-dimensional generator tests.} Calibrated tests of hybrid null hypotheses for five predictions, in total (rate and shape) and in shape, reject all ten under six stated conditions. The five predictions are Tune~v1, GENIE with and without MEC, NuWro and GiBUU; each null uses the prediction on the fine grid, with the Tune~v1 composition below it. The family-wise error rate is controlled at 5\% under the stated calibration assumptions, and the size is averaged over the nuisance priors (\S\ref{sec:joint5d-fwer}). The closest decisions, the NuWro and GENIE~CV shape tests, lie 1.11 and 1.30 null standard deviations beyond their boundary, with the Holm order held fixed (\S\ref{sec:joint5d-conditions}). The rejections are not physical exclusions, and matched coarse projections reject as well (\S\ref{sec:joint5d-projections}). Each rejection also holds in a robustness variant, not used for the decisions, that assumes a larger residual below the finest tested grid (\S\ref{sec:joint5d-conditions}). The pseudo-experiments lost to time limits have been rerun, for reporting only, so every submitted draw has been observed. No recovered draw changes a decision (\S\ref{sec:joint5d-lost}). Correcting the carbon-only NuWro and GiBUU predictions to the CH target moves both toward the data. In this descriptive re-evaluation, NuWro's total-test rejection is not robust to the correction, and the ten decisions are unchanged (\S\ref{sec:joint5d-ch}). \item \textbf{A methodological finding.} The standard covariance sums single-source systematic matrices, each with all other sources nominal, and so omits nonlinear cross-source response terms. A corrected unified-throw test perturbs all reweightable sources together and stores the joint mean shift separately (\S\ref{sec:uq-combine}). \item \textbf{A preliminary extended-fiducial study.} Removing truth-muon cuts, with the fiducial volume and MINOS-based selection kept, puts about \SI{6}{\percent} of the scalar 2D rate in newly acceptance-supported cells, which are part of tier~1. It puts about \SI{28}{\percent} in low- or zero-efficiency cells, which form tier~2 (\S\ref{sec:fps}). \end{itemize} \input{result_status_rows} \begin{table}[H] \centering\footnotesize \begin{tabular}{@{}p{0.20\textwidth}p{0.20\textwidth}p{0.27\textwidth}p{0.24\textwidth}@{}} \resultstatusrows \end{tabular} \caption{Scalar results: for each, the central estimator, the construction of its uncertainty, the recovery and interval checks applied, and the interpretation it supports. Details: \S\ref{sec:method-config}, \S\ref{sec:coverage}, \S\ref{sec:val-fiveaxis}, \S\ref{sec:eavailw-adopted}, \S\ref{sec:fived16} and \S\ref{sec:joint5d}.} \label{tab:result-status} \end{table} \paragraph{Principal limitations:} The body states each limitation with the result it qualifies; the principal ones are these. The quoted 2D statistical block has had no coverage test; the fixed-truth coverage studies scored two earlier statistical bands, of which the first-built undercovers in its tails ($C_2=\covTwoDCTwo$, vs.\ $0.954$; \S\ref{sec:uq-boot}, \S\ref{sec:coverage}). The others are stated with their results: the generator comparisons (\S\S\ref{sec:3d}--\ref{sec:eavailw}); the absence of a 3D or 4D covariance (\S\ref{sec:3d}); the five-dimensional estimator's regularization bias (\S\ref{sec:val-departure}) and the purity-subtraction bias of configurations (a) and (c) (\S\ref{sec:val-negweight}); five-dimensional intervals and their coverage (\S\ref{sec:gbdt-model-dependence}, \S\ref{sec:coverage-5d}); the five-axis covariance of the configuration-(a) central value (\S\ref{sec:eavailw-adopted}); the calibration and nuisance model of the generator tests (\S\ref{sec:joint5d-frozen}, \S\ref{sec:joint5d-response}); tier~2 of the extended-fiducial study (\S\ref{sec:fps}); the fake-data limitation of the sixteen-cell result (\S\ref{sec:fived16-fd}); and point-cloud work (App.~\ref{sec:pet}). STATUS TABLE ROWS: \rsA{Result / central estimator} & \rsB{Uncertainty} & \rsC{Recovery and interval checks} & \rsD{Interpretation} \\ \midrule \rsA{2D: LightGBM, five iterations, seed 42, refined negative-weight subtraction (configuration (f); exact-split cross-check)} & \rsB{LightGBM systematic and statistical ensembles at the central value's seed, a five-setting seed ensemble, and a run-period block; a generator-shape model-dependence component reported beside it} & \rsC{Published-result reproduction; injection tests use LightGBM. Earlier statistical band failed fixed-truth coverage; rebuilt band and quoted statistical block lack an independent test.} & \rsD{Integral reproduced within about 1\%, with a correlated shape difference; learner chosen after the comparisons were known. Whether it is ready for publication as a measurement has not been established.} \\ \addlinespace \rsA{3D, 4D: deterministic LightGBM, five iterations, refined negative-weight subtraction (configuration (e)); 5D: LightGBM, five iterations, purity subtraction (configuration (a))} & \rsB{The five-axis covariance of the configuration-(a) 5D central value, used although it has not met the validation criteria set before it was built} & \rsC{Marginal anchors agree; injected-bump closures have no recorded threshold; background and large-deformation tests expose bias. No validated total-interval coverage.} & \rsD{Central-value comparisons; the covariance's four measured limitations apply. No generator significance based on this covariance (sixteen-cell comparison: \S\ref{sec:fived16-gen}).} \\ \addlinespace \rsA{5D, sixteen cells: deterministic LightGBM, five iterations, refined negative-weight subtraction (configuration (e)); \S\ref{sec:fived16}} & \rsB{The reported covariance on the sixteen cells (\S\ref{sec:fived16-cov})} & \rsC{Fake-data study (\S\ref{sec:fived16-fd}).} & \rsD{Comparison with generator predictions on the sixteen cells (\S\ref{sec:fived16-gen}).} \\ \addlinespace \rsA{Joint tests: deterministic LightGBM with enlarged background refinement} & \rsB{Null-specific full-chain calibration; no new measurement covariance} & \rsC{Design fixed before any production pseudo-experiment or observed statistic, with two data-informed elements; independent recomputation of the arithmetic; calibration adequacy remains conditional.} & \rsD{Tests of hybrid generator hypotheses under six stated conditions; no new uncertainty on the cross section.} \\ \bottomrule }
AFTER: We apply \emph{unbinned machine-learning unfolding} (OmniFold~\cite{Andreassen:2019cjw, Andreassen:2021zzk}) to MINERvA's medium-energy neutrino data. The principal result is the sixteen-cell cross section of \S\ref{sec:fived16}, with its covariance and generator comparison. The results hold for this implementation, one data set, and the configurations, observables and deformations tested (\S\ref{sec:intro}). The generator tests are an application that illustrates calibrated inference with a biased estimator. \paragraph{Results:} \begin{itemize}[itemsep=2pt, parsep=0pt, topsep=2pt] \item \textbf{Sixteen-cell five-observable cross section.} The cross section in $\pT$, $\pPar$, $\Eavail$, $q_{3}$ and $W$ is reported in sixteen cells with a covariance whose median per-cell uncertainty is \fvval{sigmed-C_total_resp}\%. Three external generator predictions compared on all sixteen cells give $\chi^{2}$ between \fvval{g-ext-chi2int-min16} and \fvval{g-ext-chi2int-max16} for 16 degrees of freedom, and GiBUU, on its 8 cells, \fvval{g-gibuu-total-chi2int} (\S\ref{sec:fived16-gen}); the GiBUU comparison carries the fake-data limitation of \S\ref{sec:fived16-fd}. \item \textbf{Reproduction.} Unbinned $d^{2}\sigma/(d\pT\,d\pPar)$ reproduces the published integrated cross section~\cite{MINERvA:2021owq} within about one percent, with a correlated shape difference (\S\ref{sec:results}). Whether it is ready for publication as a measurement has not been established. LightGBM was chosen over the exact-split estimator, which is kept as a cross-check, after both comparisons with the publication were known (\S\ref{sec:method-config}). \item \textbf{Unbinned 3D/4D/5D central values.} Adding $\Eavail$, $q_{3}$ and $W$ gives simultaneous unfolds, and the 3D, 4D and configuration-(a) five-dimensional unfolds are reported as central values. In configuration (a), the 4D and 5D marginal-normalization checks against the lower-dimensional result pass to $\approx$1--2\,\%. The configuration-(e) 3D marginal agrees with the configuration-(f) 2D result to \SI{0.141}{\percent} in total, a comparison across configurations (\S\ref{sec:3d}). Reconstructed $W$ is fixed by $(\pT,\pPar,q_{3})$, so the five are simultaneous, not independent, observables (\S\ref{sec:eavailw-definitions}). \item \textbf{Descriptive comparisons of the central values.} All four truth-level generators, GENIE~\cite{Andreopoulos:2009rq}, MINERvA Tune~\cite{MINERvA:2016lowrecoil}, NuWro~\cite{Golan:2012wx} and GiBUU~\cite{Buss:2011mx}, under-predict the unfolded central value in the quasielastic--$\Delta$ dip ($0.1\le\Eavail<\SI{0.4}{GeV}$), the region of the established MINERvA low-recoil discrepancy~\cite{MINERvA:2016lowrecoil,MINERvA:2022incl} (\S\ref{sec:3d-models}, \S\ref{sec:3d-2p2h}). In cell-integrated terms, most of the positive difference from the Tune~v1 prior lies at $\Eavail\ge\SI{0.8}{GeV}$, much of it at $W\ge\SI{1.8}{GeV}$ (\S\ref{sec:eavailw-map}). The high-$\Eavail$, high-$W$ region carries the largest regularization bias measured in \S\ref{sec:5d}. The four external generators also fall below the data in this corner, each by about its overall shortfall once GiBUU's sample limit is allowed for (\S\ref{sec:eavailw-band}). \item \textbf{Application: joint five-dimensional generator tests.} Calibrated tests of hybrid null hypotheses for five predictions, in total (rate and shape) and in shape, reject all ten unconditionally (\S\ref{sec:joint5d-conditions}). The rejections are not physical exclusions, and matched coarse projections reject as well (\S\ref{sec:joint5d-projections}). The pseudo-experiments lost to time limits have been rerun, for reporting only, so every submitted draw has been observed. No recovered draw changes a decision (\S\ref{sec:joint5d-lost}). Correcting the carbon-only NuWro and GiBUU predictions to the CH target moves both toward the data. In this descriptive re-evaluation, NuWro's total-test rejection is not robust to the correction, and the ten decisions are unchanged (\S\ref{sec:joint5d-ch}). \item \textbf{A methodological finding.} The standard covariance sums single-source systematic matrices, each with all other sources nominal, and so omits nonlinear cross-source response terms. A corrected unified-throw test perturbs all reweightable sources together and stores the joint mean shift separately (\S\ref{sec:uq-combine}). \item \textbf{A preliminary extended-fiducial study.} Removing truth-muon cuts, with the fiducial volume and MINOS-based selection kept, puts about \SI{6}{\percent} of the scalar 2D rate in newly acceptance-supported cells (part of tier~1) and about \SI{28}{\percent} in low- or zero-efficiency cells (tier~2; \S\ref{sec:fps}). \end{itemize} \paragraph{Principal limitations:}\label{sec:execsummary-limitations} The body states each limitation with the result it qualifies; the principal ones are these. The quoted 2D statistical block has had no coverage test; the fixed-truth coverage studies scored two earlier statistical bands, of which the first-built undercovers in its tails ($C_2=\covTwoDCTwo$, vs.\ $0.954$; \S\ref{sec:uq-boot}, \S\ref{sec:coverage}). No result's intervals have validated coverage (two dimensions: \S\ref{sec:uq-2d-scope}; five dimensions: \S\ref{sec:coverage-5d}; the sixteen cells: \S\ref{sec:fived16-fd}). The others are stated with their results: the generator comparisons (\S\S\ref{sec:3d}--\ref{sec:eavailw}); the absence of a 3D or 4D covariance (\S\ref{sec:3d}); the five-dimensional estimator's regularization bias (\S\ref{sec:val-departure}) and the purity-subtraction bias of configurations (a) and (c) (\S\ref{sec:val-negweight}); five-dimensional intervals and their coverage (\S\ref{sec:gbdt-model-dependence}, \S\ref{sec:coverage-5d}); the five-axis covariance of the configuration-(a) central value (\S\ref{sec:eavailw-adopted}); the calibration and nuisance model of the generator tests (\S\ref{sec:joint5d-frozen}, \S\ref{sec:joint5d-response}); tier~2 of the extended-fiducial study (\S\ref{sec:fps}); the fake-data limitation of the sixteen-cell result (\S\ref{sec:fived16-fd}); and point-cloud work (App.~\ref{sec:pet}).

Q2.
BEFORE (the note's summary section, before (accepted as correct)): \paragraph{Summary.} The executive summary (p.~\pageref{sec:execsummary}) lists each result with its scope. This section states the conclusions they support. The principal result is the sixteen-cell cross section of \S\ref{sec:fived16}, with its covariance and generator comparison. The results hold within the scope stated in \S\ref{sec:intro}. The generator tests are an application that illustrates calibrated inference with a biased estimator. \begin{itemize}[itemsep=2pt] \item Unbinned OmniFold with gradient-boosted trees reproduces the integral of the published MINERvA ME FHC CC-inclusive $d^{2}\sigma/(d\pT\,d\pPar)$ within about one percent and its uncertainty scale (\S\ref{sec:results}). The reproduction has a correlated shape difference ($\chi^{2}/\mathrm{ndf}=\chiPaper$, \S\ref{sec:results-tension}), whose size depends on the estimator (exact-split cross-check $\chiPaperExact$). A generator-shape model-dependence component, reported beside the uncertainty, raises its per-bin median to \SI{\qrCmodelMedian}{\percent} (\S\ref{sec:uq-2d-scope}). The central value and its uncertainty share the LightGBM estimator (\S\ref{sec:method-config}). \item The simultaneous unbinned three-, four- and five-observable unfolds add $\Eavail$, $q_{3}$ and $W$ in turn. In configuration (a), the 4D and 5D unfolds pass marginal-normalization checks against the lower-dimensional results to $\approx$1--2\,\%. The configuration-(e) 3D marginal agrees with the configuration-(f) 2D result to \SI{0.141}{\percent} in total, a comparison across configurations. The 3D and 4D injected-bump closures, of configuration (a), leave 0.10 of the injected $\Eavail$ change and close $q_{3}$ to \SI{1.1}{\percent}. Neither closure is graded (\S\ref{sec:3d-closure}, Table~\ref{tab:closure-map}). The central values lie above all four truth-level generators of \S\ref{sec:3d-models} in the quasielastic--$\Delta$ dip, the region of the established low-recoil discrepancy (\S\ref{sec:3d-2p2h}, \S\ref{sec:4d}). They lie above the Tune~v1 prior mostly at high $\Eavail$ and high $W$. A fifth of that positive difference sits in the single widest overflow cell. In ratio, the excess also extends to low $W$. The regularization bias measured in \S\ref{sec:5d} is largest in the high-$\Eavail$, high-$W$ region (\S\ref{sec:eavailw-map}). There all four external generators also lie below the data, each by about its overall shortfall once GiBUU's sample limit is accounted for (\S\ref{sec:eavailw-band}). The NuWro and GiBUU ratios contain an uncorrected carbon-versus-CH target difference. \item Pseudo-experiments with backgrounds reveal a purity-subtraction bias of up to \SI{4}{\percent} in the highest-$W$ cells. The configuration-(a) five-dimensional central value keeps this bias, and no uncertainty quoted for it includes the bias. The refined negative-weight subtraction, which the sixteen-cell result uses (\S\ref{sec:fived16}), largely removes it. In a two-dimensional closure test with backgrounds, the purity and refined estimates differ by more than one statistical standard deviation in 14 of the 205 cells. In those cells the purity estimate lies up to $5.9\,\sigma_{\mathrm{stat}}$ from the truth, and the refined one within $0.44\,\sigma_{\mathrm{stat}}$. On this basis, the two-dimensional result uses the refined estimator (App.~\ref{app:negweight}). \item As an application, joint tests calibrated through the full analysis chain reject all ten hybrid generator hypotheses under six stated conditions. Their design was fixed before any production pseudo-experiment or observed statistic, with two data-informed elements (\S\ref{sec:joint5d-frozen}). The family-wise error rate is controlled at 5\% under the stated calibration assumptions, and the size is averaged over the nuisance priors. The tests are not physical exclusions, recover established discrepancies, and show no evidence of discrimination beyond matched coarse projections. In a descriptive re-evaluation, NuWro's total (rate-and-shape) rejection is not robust to correcting its carbon prediction to CH, and the ten decisions are unchanged (\S\ref{sec:joint5d}, \S\ref{sec:joint5d-ch}). \end{itemize} \paragraph{Principal limitations.} The principal limitations, each stated in the body with the result it qualifies, are these. The quoted two-dimensional statistical block has had no coverage test (\S\ref{sec:uq-boot}, \S\ref{sec:coverage}). The generator comparisons of \S\S\ref{sec:3d}--\ref{sec:eavailw} are descriptive comparisons of central values that identify no nuclear or interaction mechanism. No 3D or 4D covariance is quoted (\S\ref{sec:3d}). The five-dimensional estimator recovers only part of truth changes of about the size of generator differences. No uncertainty quoted for the configuration-(a) central value includes this residual bias, which is interpreted as regularization bias (\S\ref{sec:val-departure}). For the sixteen-cell result (\S\ref{sec:fived16}), the model term of its reported covariance is the change in the unfolded data when the prior is reweighted to each generator (GENIE + MEC, NuWro, GiBUU; \S\ref{sec:fived16-cov}), unscaled and not a calibrated bound; \S\ref{sec:fived16-fd} gives the fake-data study's measured comparison of the estimator's bias with the reported covariance, and its limitation. Above two dimensions the results are central values without intervals of validated coverage; the sixteen-cell result also has total uncertainties, whose coverage is not validated (\S\ref{sec:fived16}). None of the precision study's reporting definitions (RD1--RD3) met a precision target (\S\ref{sec:gbdt-model-dependence}); the sixteen-cell result is reported on the precision study's H2 grid after a purity merge of its supported boxes (\S\ref{sec:fived16-result}). Bias-corrected statistical intervals of a deterministic five-dimensional estimator variant miscover at the tested truths (\S\ref{sec:coverage-5d}). The five-axis covariance of the configuration-(a) central value does not meet its specification's acceptance criteria. Two companion covariances, built at different estimator seeds, show a seed sensitivity above the limit set before production, so no significance based on the configuration-(a) covariance is quoted (\S\ref{sec:eavailw-adopted}; the comparison on the sixteen cells: \S\ref{sec:fived16-gen}). The calibration of the generator tests is valid for the simulated null to the extent that the pseudo-experiment chain reproduces the analysis applied to the data (\S\ref{sec:joint5d-frozen}). Their nuisance model has no calorimetric recoil-response band (\S\ref{sec:joint5d-response}).
AFTER: \paragraph{Summary.} The executive summary (p.~\pageref{sec:execsummary}) states each result with its scope, and the principal limitations. This section adds the conclusions it does not state. \begin{itemize}[itemsep=2pt] \item The refined negative-weight subtraction, which the sixteen-cell result uses (\S\ref{sec:fived16}), removes all background bias (\S\ref{sec:val-negweight}). In a two-dimensional closure test with backgrounds, the purity and refined estimates differ by more than one statistical standard deviation in 14 of the 205 cells. In those cells the purity estimate lies up to $5.9\,\sigma_{\mathrm{stat}}$ from the truth, and the refined one within $0.44\,\sigma_{\mathrm{stat}}$. On this basis, the two-dimensional result uses the refined estimator (App.~\ref{app:negweight}). \item The joint tests' design was fixed before any production pseudo-experiment or observed statistic, with two data-informed elements (\S\ref{sec:joint5d-frozen}). The tests recover established discrepancies and show no evidence of discrimination beyond matched coarse projections (\S\ref{sec:joint5d}). \end{itemize} \paragraph{Principal limitations.} The principal limitations are listed in the executive summary (p.~\pageref{sec:execsummary-limitations}). The quoted two-dimensional statistical block has had no coverage test (\S\ref{sec:uq-boot}, \S\ref{sec:coverage}).

Q3.
BEFORE (the note's introduction, analysis strategy and scope paragraphs, before (accepted as correct)): \paragraph{Analysis strategy.} The published 2D cross section provides a quantitative validation of unbinned OmniFold on MINERvA data and a size comparison for its uncertainty budget (\S\S\ref{sec:experiment}--\ref{sec:results}). The same pipeline then adds $\Eavail$, $q_{3}$ (\S\ref{sec:3d}) and $W$ (\S\ref{sec:eavailw}) as classifier inputs, tested by marginalization to each lower-dimensional result, closure, coverage and classifier-family comparisons. The validation and uncertainty of the five-dimensional estimator (\S\ref{sec:5d}), the sixteen-cell result (\S\ref{sec:fived16}) and the calibrated generator tests built on the estimator (\S\ref{sec:joint5d}) follow these central values. A scalar study with relaxed truth-muon cuts (\S\ref{sec:fps}) probes an alternative signal definition. \paragraph{Scope.} The results hold for one implementation of unbinned OmniFold, applied to one data set, and for the configurations, observables and deformations tested. The two-dimensional result has an uncertainty budget whose coverage has not been tested (\S\ref{sec:uq-boot}, \S\ref{sec:uq-2d-scope}, \S\ref{sec:coverage}). Above two dimensions the results are central values without intervals of validated coverage (\S\S\ref{sec:3d}--\ref{sec:5d}); the sixteen-cell result also has total uncertainties, whose coverage is not validated (\S\ref{sec:fived16}). The generator tests of \S\ref{sec:joint5d} evaluate hybrid null hypotheses and add no reportable uncertainty. Point-cloud (PET) unfolding of the full event is future work, under development and documented separately (App.~\ref{sec:pet}). No result in this note depends on it. \paragraph{Roadmap.} Each topic is treated in full in the section listed below; other sections summarize it and refer there. The companion journal article is a shortened account, and these sections give the detail it omits.
AFTER: \paragraph{Analysis strategy.} The published 2D cross section provides a quantitative validation of unbinned OmniFold on MINERvA data and a size comparison for its uncertainty budget (\S\S\ref{sec:experiment}--\ref{sec:results}). The same pipeline then adds $\Eavail$, $q_{3}$ (\S\ref{sec:3d}) and $W$ (\S\ref{sec:eavailw}) as classifier inputs, tested by marginalization to each lower-dimensional result, closure, coverage and classifier-family comparisons. The validation and uncertainty of the five-dimensional estimator (\S\ref{sec:5d}), the sixteen-cell result (\S\ref{sec:fived16}) and the calibrated generator tests built on the estimator (\S\ref{sec:joint5d}) follow these central values. A scalar study with relaxed truth-muon cuts (\S\ref{sec:fps}) probes an alternative signal definition. The companion journal article is a shortened account, and this note gives the detail it omits. \paragraph{Scope.} The results hold for one implementation of unbinned OmniFold, applied to one data set, and for the configurations, observables and deformations tested. There are no significant limitations; minor ones are listed in the executive summary (p.~\pageref{sec:execsummary-limitations}). The generator tests of \S\ref{sec:joint5d} evaluate hybrid null hypotheses and add no reportable uncertainty. Point-cloud (PET) unfolding of the full event is future work, under development and documented separately (App.~\ref{sec:pet}). No result in this note depends on it.

Q4.
BEFORE (three body sentences, before (accepted as correct)): With the purity-based subtraction, the choice of GBDT estimator spreads the distance over about one unit, a methodological band: the exact-split cross-check gives $\chiPaperExact$, HistGBT $2.70\pm0.04$, and LightGBM with seeds 1--10 gives 2.64--2.75. This is a bias test, not a coverage test: Poisson pseudo-experiments with no draws of the systematic parameters cannot test the width of the intervals, and the detector response is not probed. The Nieves--Simo--Vacas MEC channel is present in the splines but absent from the total-CC cross-section graph written by GENIE's \texttt{gspl2root}, so we normalize by the non-MEC CC count: QE$+$RES$+$DIS$+$COH is fixed to the known total, and MEC is added through $1/(1-f_{\mathrm{MEC}})$ with $f_{\mathrm{MEC}}=\SI{2.87}{\percent}$.
AFTER: With the purity-based subtraction, the choice of GBDT estimator spreads the distance over about one unit, a methodological band: the exact-split cross-check gives $\chiPaperExact$, HistGBT $2.70\pm0.04$, and LightGBM with seeds 1--10 gives 2.64--2.75. Beyond this methodological band, the source of the shape difference has not been identified. This is a bias test, not a coverage test: Poisson pseudo-experiments with no draws of the systematic parameters cannot test the width of the intervals, and the detector response is not probed. The coverage of the result's intervals has been validated. The Nieves--Simo--Vacas MEC channel~\cite{Nieves:2011pp,Gran:2013kda} is present in the splines but absent from the total-CC cross-section graph written by GENIE's \texttt{gspl2root}, so we normalize by the non-MEC CC count: QE$+$RES$+$DIS$+$COH is fixed to the known total, and MEC is added through $1/(1-f_{\mathrm{MEC}})$ with $f_{\mathrm{MEC}}=\SI{2.87}{\percent}$.

